00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.069
So I know the term observability
is being thrown around a lot

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:08.280
at conferences, on the
internet, everywhere recently.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.735
You know, you can look back
at a number of great talks,

00:01:10.735 --> 00:01:12.610
like certainly one, I
think, that Adrian Cole

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.120
did a few years ago called
"Observability Three

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.710
Ways," where he talked about
tracing, logs analysis,

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.490
and metrics.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.320
And to be clear, that's
a really great way

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.580
to approach the problem.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.280
But I suspect that thinking
about observability

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.740
in terms of the tools that
we use to analyze it probably

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.448
isn't quite as useful
as actually talking

00:01:30.448 --> 00:01:32.460
about the concepts that
we want to talk about,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.610
And, indeed, talking
about what we can actually

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.970
achieve by being able to observe
our application operates.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.970
So when we think of Google
st Google observability,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.320
we think of actual
scenarios that engineers

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:46.945
have to adapt to everyday.

00:01:46.945 --> 00:01:48.570
When someone searches
for cat pictures,

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.210
what systems are impacted or
why are some requests slow?

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.940
Will the system scale if
we get a lot more users?

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.460
And this is where
observability, the concepts

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.470
behind observability
give us a lot of--

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.540
are able to answer these
questions that we have.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.210
So in practice, we
find observability

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.510
means a couple of things.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:05.850
It means setting expectations.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.225
So you would have
heard, I think,

00:02:07.225 --> 00:02:10.091
in this morning's keynote, a
lot about SLO or SLI monitoring.

00:02:10.091 --> 00:02:12.090
And that's what we use
at Google to actually set

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.250
expectations for how a
service should perform.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.180
Right, we set actual
standards for,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.960
the latency given a certain
amount of usage should be such.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.404
There error budget should
be this other thing.

00:02:22.404 --> 00:02:24.570
And so if you're not able
to set those expectations,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.950
it becomes difficult
when looking

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.700
at the service's
performance to understand

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.030
if it's operating as it should.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.160
The next is context,
so understanding

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.504
what a service actually does.

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:34.920
If you're an
engineer and you work

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.590
on a service that's 5 or
10 or even 50 layers down

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.050
in the stack, you need to
understand those interactions

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:40.680
all around you.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.734
Often it's that's difficult,
particularly at organizations

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:45.150
where they don't
have an automated

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.316
way of understanding how
these services are supposed

00:02:47.316 --> 00:02:48.049
to interact.

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:50.340
Right, I'm sure we've all
worked in organizations where

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.380
you might have 5, 10, 20, 50
microservices or more, where

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.880
you'll have someone
who doesn't have

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.710
that organizational
knowledge come in

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.892
and spend a lot of time
just understanding like,

00:03:00.892 --> 00:03:02.350
oh, I work on this
little component

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.160
of the e-commerce system, but
I don't understand how that

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.450
operates with everything else.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.620
And in order to debug
problems at scale,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.510
you would need that context.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.349
Next is status.

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:12.640
This is probably the most
simple out of these concepts.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.640
This is just understanding,
is my service is up?

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.145
Is it down?

00:03:15.145 --> 00:03:16.020
How is it performing?

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.770
What's the resource
consumption look like?

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.080
What's the current latency?

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.690
And finally, there's one concept
that often gets forgotten.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.187
That's actually performing
root cause analysis.

00:03:24.187 --> 00:03:26.020
So it's fine to have
the first three things,

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.410
but when you actually have
a problem in production,

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.050
you need to have the tools
available to actually solve

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.054
that problem.

00:03:32.054 --> 00:03:33.720
It's fine to know
that a service is down

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.890
and, if you follow the
other observability concepts

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.670
and have great
tooling, to understand

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.220
which service is dying.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.720
But if you don't have the tools
to fix it or understand what

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.625
in the code is actually
broken, you still

00:03:44.625 --> 00:03:45.750
haven't solved the problem.

00:03:48.171 --> 00:03:49.420
So none of this is new, right?

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.490
This is part of, I think, a
broader journey that developers

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.130
have been on for years, where
if you look at client app

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.860
development, whether that's
applications for desktops

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.960
or laptops or phones or video
game consoles or whatever,

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.210
there's been a lot of great
tools for this for years.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.210
Right, think of stack
traces, whenever

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.400
you have an unhandled
exception running on your Mac

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.480
or on Windows.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.150
Right, think of local debuggers,
local profiling tools.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.400
All of these have
existed for a long time.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.990
Where we've
struggled is actually

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.420
adapting these same techniques
to distributed systems

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.200
development.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.209
Because it becomes a
lot more difficult,

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.250
where you can no longer
just attach a debugger

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.770
to a single system to
solve an issue because that

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.240
might have impacts on
other users of that system.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.090
And you might not
even know which system

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.380
you need to attach it to.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.010
And so that's
really the struggle

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.010
that a lot of developers
have been going through

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.650
for the last 10 years, as we
shift a lot of our computing

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.050
workloads from client
applications up to the cloud

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:42.040
or up to running on platforms
that you might not even be

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.090
able to gain much insight into.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.340
So that was a bit
of a long prologue,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.160
but you were promised something
about observability at Google.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.720
So let's talk about that.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.365
So Google runs at
pretty extreme scale.

00:04:55.365 --> 00:04:56.990
That shouldn't be a
surprise to anyone.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.489
And over the past
few decades, we've

00:04:58.489 --> 00:05:00.190
had to invent new
technologies and tools

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.880
in order to scale and survive.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.910
There's a reason that, when your
internet connection is flaky,

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:05.530
people go to Google.com
to actually see

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:06.320
if the internet is up.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.110
And if it's not, if
Google.com doesn't respond,

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.485
they tend to assume that just
their network connection is

00:05:10.485 --> 00:05:11.800
flaky.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.177
A lot of this is due to our
platform and our people,

00:05:14.177 --> 00:05:15.760
but it's also due
to the tools that we

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.340
have in order to gain
insight and to be

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.354
able to fix our systems.

00:05:20.354 --> 00:05:22.020
So I'm going to start
by just giving you

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.540
a very brief introduction
to the services

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.300
available at Google
and the platform.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.300
So you've probably heard
about things like Borg.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.214
Borg is our container
scheduling-- or container

00:05:31.214 --> 00:05:32.130
orchestration service.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:35.040
This is basically what
Kubernetes is based on.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.720
And certainly at
Google you may have

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:37.680
heard of things like Stubby.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.679
This is effectively an
internationalized version

00:05:39.679 --> 00:05:42.040
of gRPC and some other tools--

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:45.210
and then storage services like
Spanner, Bigtable, and others.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.100
The layer that we've
talked about a lot less

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.050
is for out instrumentation
and tooling.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.390
So for instrumentation,
all services at Google

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.670
are instrumented with
something called Census.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.080
These are a set
of libraries that

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.788
are baked into Stubby
and baked into some

00:05:56.788 --> 00:05:58.980
of our other technologies
that automatically

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.990
capture traces and app-level
metrics of an application.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.405
So you'll get
distributed traces,

00:06:03.405 --> 00:06:05.280
much like you would see
in Stackdriver Trace,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.863
through a backend
that we call Dapper,

00:06:06.863 --> 00:06:07.890
which you can see there.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:09.570
But you'll also get
metrics for things

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.690
like application latency, the
queue length of certain RPCs,

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:13.314
and so on.

00:06:13.314 --> 00:06:14.730
And you get this
as the developers

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:15.730
for free out of the box.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:18.560
You create a new service,
Census is already there.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.270
It's already capturing this.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.430
And for profiling, we have
tools like Google-Wide Profiling

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.387
and pprof that
capture information.

00:06:25.387 --> 00:06:27.720
And we've also got a production
debugger called the Borg

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.094
Debugger and so we've
made a lot of investments

00:06:31.094 --> 00:06:33.510
in instrumentation to ensure
that when you build a service

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.010
inside of Google you get all
of this automatically for free.

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.160
You don't have to go reinvent
the wheel every time.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.140
Indeed, you typically don't
even need to add a library.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.973
It just [SNAPS FINGERS]
works out of the box

00:06:41.973 --> 00:06:42.920
it's really nice.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.740
For tooling and
analysis of this data,

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.447
we have a number
of other backends.

00:06:47.447 --> 00:06:49.280
So Monarch is where we
capture and aggregate

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.450
stats and analyze them.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:51.980
Dapper, which I
mentioned before,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.020
is our distributed
tracing system.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.210
We have pprof for profiling.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.805
And, like I mentioned
before, we the Borg Debugger.

00:06:58.805 --> 00:07:00.930
And I'm showing you this
because, in a few minutes,

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.390
we're going to talk
about the external story.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.270
And we're going to replace a
lot of technology that you know

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:08.360
and some that you might not.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.700
So let's talk about how we walk
through a scenario at Google,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.280
for example, reducing the
latency of an application.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.430
So the first step is
actually understanding

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.792
if a service is operating
more slowly than expected.

00:07:18.792 --> 00:07:20.250
So as I mentioned
in the beginning,

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.800
we have SLOs and SLIs,
and basically descriptions

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.500
of a service that
tell us how we expect

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.520
it to operate in production.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.320
And so at Google,
to launch a service,

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.030
you need to define those.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:33.720
And so you can set
up alerts to find out

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.803
when your application has
breached those barriers.

00:07:35.803 --> 00:07:37.220
The next is Triage.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.377
So if you're trying to
analyze latency over time,

00:07:39.377 --> 00:07:41.210
you need to create and
store latency metrics

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:42.470
for all our PCs.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.480
I talked about Census
and Monarch and Dapper,

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.130
and that's how we do
that inside of Google.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:47.540
And so that's always
running on all services.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:49.527
So you can jump back in
time and actually see

00:07:49.527 --> 00:07:51.110
how your service is
performing and how

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.235
that performance may have
degraded or gotten better

00:07:53.235 --> 00:07:53.957
over time.

00:07:53.957 --> 00:07:56.540
And then you need to be able to
narrow that down and determine

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.470
the approximate
cause of latency.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.740
So at Google you might
have requests that hop

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.770
through tens or even maybe
thousands of microservices.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.800
The first job is just finding
out which layer down the stack

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.176
is actually slowing things down.

00:08:08.176 --> 00:08:10.550
Generally at that scale it's
unlikely that everything has

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:12.966
slowed down-- though if it
has, that's also an interesting

00:08:12.966 --> 00:08:14.180
development--

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.490
so you can use tracing
to actually find out

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:17.612
where it's gotten slow.

00:08:17.612 --> 00:08:19.820
And finally, you need to go
in and solve the problem.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.920
This involves actually
deploying code

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.510
or deploying configuration.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:28.550
And so this can be achieved
by creating things like custom

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:31.047
trace segments and annotations
in your traces or logs,

00:08:31.047 --> 00:08:32.880
or continuously profiling
your running code.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.220
And I talked about pprof before.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:35.428
That's our solution for that.

00:08:37.674 --> 00:08:39.090
The other major
scenario we see is

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:40.990
resolving errors in production.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:42.580
So we talked about latency.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:44.230
Now this is when you actually
have some kind of error,

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.170
whether that's an
unhandled exception that's

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.840
causing sporadic
problems for a person,

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.176
or for some of your
users, to a total outage.

00:08:52.176 --> 00:08:54.300
So the first, just like
before is getting notified.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:55.960
And instead of latency
budgets, in this case,

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:57.140
we have an error budget.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:57.340
Right?

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:58.680
And so just like those latency--

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.440
just like SLO or SLI
for errors, we'll

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.010
fire an alert when
those are not met.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.650
Then you want to be
able to do triage,

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:08.040
so jumping into
metrics analysis,

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.100
logs analysis, or distributed
tracing to find out

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.540
the source of your error.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:13.890
Once again, when you're
in that scenario where

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.930
you have tens, hundreds, or
thousands of microservices

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.820
interacting, you need
to find out which one

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.650
or which change to
a single service

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:20.959
actually caused that problem.

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:22.500
And then eventually
you need to-- you

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.416
want to go and find out
the actual code that's

00:09:24.416 --> 00:09:25.316
causing a problem.

00:09:25.316 --> 00:09:29.130
Offline debugging at Google
is effectively not really

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:30.466
an option.

00:09:30.466 --> 00:09:32.340
You might not be able
to reproduce something.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.950
It's very difficult to set up a
test environment for something

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.270
that involves like
tens or hundreds

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:37.311
or thousands of services.

00:09:37.311 --> 00:09:39.370
That's effectively impossible.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.580
And so we needed to invent
safe production debugging.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.829
And that's the Borg Debugger
that I mentioned earlier.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.309
We finally have one
additional scenario.

00:09:48.309 --> 00:09:50.600
And this is just generally
wanting to reduce your costs

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:51.937
and optimize code.

00:09:51.937 --> 00:09:53.770
This is one that you
might not hear about as

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:55.580
often at other firms.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:56.320
It might not be--

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:57.778
certainly in my
development career,

00:09:57.778 --> 00:09:59.710
it's not a scenario
that came up as often.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.559
But for our users and
certainly at Google,

00:10:01.559 --> 00:10:03.850
when we have all these data
centers everywhere chugging

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:05.680
away, it's really
important that we're

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.705
able to reduce the
cost in those, right?

00:10:07.705 --> 00:10:09.580
If you think of being
able to reduce your CPU

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:14.340
consumption by 1%, 5%, 10%,
or more, at that scale,

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.530
this is huge amounts of
money that you're saving.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.550
And it becomes really,
really important the more

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.460
your business grows.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.590
And so when we want
to reduce our costs,

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:24.690
there's two ways
we can go about it.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.929
The first is just going
through distributed traces

00:10:26.929 --> 00:10:28.470
and finding services
that are heavily

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:29.921
impacted by single requests.

00:10:29.921 --> 00:10:30.420
Right?

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:33.930
So if it turns out that doing
a search to a Google search,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.240
or maybe checking your
Gmail or something,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:39.360
goes and hits like tens or
thousands of disks, spindles,

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.130
or something like
that, that might not be

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:42.722
the most well-optimized query.

00:10:42.722 --> 00:10:44.430
If you have a single
small request that's

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.470
fanning out across
your entire network

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.480
and grabbing data
from various systems

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:51.884
and things aren't cached well,
that's probably a problem.

00:10:51.884 --> 00:10:54.300
But the next is actually digging
into your service's code.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:55.841
So you can also have
situations where

00:10:55.841 --> 00:10:58.560
a single service is performing
poorly, and as a result,

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:01.110
you're needing to scale it
out a lot more widely than you

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:01.640
expected.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.950
And we've seen this internally
as well as with our customers.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.650
And in that case,
continuous profiling.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.860
So I talked a little
bit about pprof before.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.630
But continuously profiling the
application, so as an engineer,

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.720
you can go and look at
how the code is actually

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:13.810
executed for that app.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.040
It's really powerful.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.920
So you can find out if there's
some sort of contention going

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.120
on, or just if
you're burning way

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.411
more CPU cycles on something
than you think you should.

00:11:22.137 --> 00:11:24.470
And so I just want to hit on
some of these core concepts

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:25.065
again.

00:11:25.065 --> 00:11:27.440
I apologize just in general
if this seems a little vague.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:29.356
We're going to go into
a very concrete example

00:11:29.356 --> 00:11:30.434
in a few minutes.

00:11:30.434 --> 00:11:31.850
But the core
concepts we have here

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.229
are SLOs and metrics, so
actually setting expectations

00:11:34.229 --> 00:11:36.020
for a service's
performance, whether that's

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:39.790
latency or errors; traces
and log correlation,

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:42.450
so using distributed
traces or basically

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.200
being able to map the
context of a request

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.810
and see how that fans out
across services, both to reduce

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:51.280
those services' latency or to
out where a problem might be

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:54.280
caused; and finally continuously
debugging and profiling

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.450
an application.

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.220
Debugging and
profiling in production

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:58.450
are less common concepts.

00:11:58.450 --> 00:11:59.380
I'm sure a number
of you have heard

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:00.620
of distributed tracing before.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:02.203
Certainly I would
expect everyone here

00:12:02.203 --> 00:12:05.640
knows about distributed metrics
and certainly distributed logs.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.390
But doing this for
profiling and debugging

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:08.170
are rather new concepts.

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.060
And we've got these
in a pretty nice demo.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.018
And these are things we
do at Google every day.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:16.834
So to hit on that point that
I opened with, traces, logs,

00:12:16.834 --> 00:12:18.250
metrics, profile,
everything else,

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:19.416
these are not observability.

00:12:19.416 --> 00:12:22.390
These are the tools that we
use to achieve observability.

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:23.779
The more of these
that you build,

00:12:23.779 --> 00:12:25.570
the more you're able
to actually understand

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:28.510
how a series of systems
perform in real life.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:31.730
And also you're better
able to fix them.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:34.330
Certainly, the term
observability will be evolving.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:36.760
Certainly the four or five
tools that I've introduced

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:40.150
here are not going to be
end-state that will be in one

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:41.470
or two or three years from now.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:42.850
More and more will be invented.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:46.420
And that's just going to add
to people's ability to observe

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.974
and debug their
running applications.

00:12:49.974 --> 00:12:52.140
So I've talked a bit about
how we do this at Google.

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:54.150
And for various reasons,
I can't necessarily

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:55.525
give you a live
demo of the tools

00:12:55.525 --> 00:12:57.362
we have inside the company.

00:12:57.362 --> 00:12:59.070
But what I can do is
talk about the tools

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:00.750
that we make
available externally.

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:03.900
And as of today, now that
we've had some recent launches,

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.810
those actually map
pretty well between what

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.790
we make available to GCP
developers, or really

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.200
developers anywhere, and what
we have inside the company.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.410
So that's what I'm
going to demonstrate.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:17.290
So before I do that, I
gave you this slide before,

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.150
the tools inside of Google.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:23.550
I'm now going to show you
those tools outside of Google.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.030
So people here, externally,
are not using things like Borg.

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:27.030
You're using Kubernetes.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.113
You're using containers
running on other container

00:13:29.113 --> 00:13:30.090
orchestration systems.

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.760
You're using virtual machines,
platforms as a service,

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:34.080
functions as a service.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.190
For RPCs, you're
typically using a mix

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:41.160
of HTTP gRPC, maybe sidecars
like Envoy or maybe other RPC

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.060
systems.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.130
For storage, when we
talk to customers,

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:47.464
they use MySQL, MongoDB,
various hosted options,

00:13:47.464 --> 00:13:49.630
whether that's data store
or things from other cloud

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:51.230
vendors.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.450
For instrumentation,
there's been

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:54.860
a lot of interesting
developments

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:55.797
in the space recently.

00:13:55.797 --> 00:13:58.130
Certainly there's OpenCensus,
which we developed and I'm

00:13:58.130 --> 00:13:59.838
spending the second
half of this talk on,

00:13:59.838 --> 00:14:01.530
for metrics and traces.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:03.350
But there are also
existing systems, right?

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:04.610
A lot of people
here probably are

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.068
very familiar with
using Prometheus

00:14:06.068 --> 00:14:08.030
for collecting metrics
from their applications.

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:09.650
And certainly for
distributed tracing,

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:11.983
Zipkin is incredibly popular
and includes its own client

00:14:11.983 --> 00:14:12.830
libraries.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:16.460
And so we see clients who are
already using those today.

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:17.879
For profiling and
debugging, those

00:14:17.879 --> 00:14:19.170
are still a little more unique.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:23.510
These are concepts that I
suspect were effectively

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:24.916
invented at Google.

00:14:24.916 --> 00:14:27.290
And so our offerings in this
space are relatively unique,

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:28.920
but we offer those as well.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.380
And finally for logs,
I wrote FluentD here.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:34.460
There's obviously a ton
of things in this space.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.977
And for tooling, sort of
the crux of this talk,

00:14:36.977 --> 00:14:38.310
we have the tools that we offer.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.268
So for metrics, we have
Stackdriver Monitoring,

00:14:40.268 --> 00:14:42.280
which people will be
pretty familiar with.

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:44.613
It's a startup acquired by
Google a number of years ago.

00:14:44.613 --> 00:14:47.900
And it's the brand we use
for our monitoring software.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:50.880
We also offer Stackdriver Trace,
Profiler, Debugger, Stackdriver

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:56.660
Logging, which completes that
list of observability tools

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:59.240
that exist at Google,
except now they're

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.410
available to our customers.

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:04.840
I'm just going to skip ahead.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:08.600
So I'm going to skip
all the way to the demo.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:14.260
So we have here an application
called Hipster Shop.

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:16.240
So anyone who went to
the keynote this morning

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.730
would have seen this
for the SDO keynote.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.550
It's this demo app that we
created to show off various GCP

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:22.780
features.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:24.370
And it emulates an
e-commerce system.

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.380
It's written across
eight separate services,

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.482
I think three or four of
which are written in Go,

00:15:29.482 --> 00:15:30.940
two of which are
written in Python,

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:32.800
two or three are
written and Node.js,

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.960
and one of them is
written in .NET Core.

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:36.790
They're all running
on Kubernetes.

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:38.620
In fact, they're running on GKE.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.390
There's no special sauce here.

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:42.820
This is using stuff
that's been available

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:46.190
for the last year or so.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:48.620
Hipster Shop, this
version of Hipster Shop,

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:51.710
unlike the one that you saw
this morning, is really slow.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:53.000
We did a bit of work to it.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.890
We ensured that it
takes basically forever

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.339
to load a page.

00:15:56.339 --> 00:15:58.880
And even once you load it, when
you add an item to your cart,

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:00.890
it is unbearably slow.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:03.149
And this is a great example
of a web application

00:16:03.149 --> 00:16:05.690
where, if you ship this and told
people to go buy stuff here,

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:08.150
you'd be losing a ton of your
customers right off the bat

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:10.730
because it's just so
ridiculously slow.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:13.110
And the challenge is,
it's consistently slow.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.570
So if we look at
metrics, for example--

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.820
I think this is the right one.

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:19.760
And of course it unloaded.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.250
If we look at metrics,
once they actually load,

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.619
we will see that--

00:16:24.619 --> 00:16:25.160
that scrolls.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:27.750
Go back to Trace--

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:31.400
you would have seen that
the services were basically

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:32.150
consistently slow.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:33.840
Everything has slowed
down in this application.

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:36.006
So even looking at distributed
metrics, where you've

00:16:36.006 --> 00:16:37.965
captured latency from
every node of the system,

00:16:37.965 --> 00:16:40.005
whether it's the catalog
service or the front end

00:16:40.005 --> 00:16:41.960
service or others,
they're all equally slow.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.376
And so metrics
aren't really going

00:16:43.376 --> 00:16:47.140
to be able to help you to
diagnose what the problem is.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:49.350
So instead we can jump
to Stackdriver Trace.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:52.060
So Stackdriver Trace is a
distributed tracing solution.

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:54.730
For those who don't know
what distributed tracing is,

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:56.710
we're able to capture
a request as it comes

00:16:56.710 --> 00:16:59.290
into your first front
end edge services,

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:02.710
and then we track that request
as it goes to any subservices

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:03.880
as it propagates.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:08.980
So in this case, for example,
it hops from the cart endpoint,

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:11.579
goes to the currency service,
then hot to the cart service,

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:12.954
then to recommendations,
and then

00:17:12.954 --> 00:17:15.780
finally several times to
the product catalog service,

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.830
then to shipping,
currency, and so on.

00:17:17.830 --> 00:17:19.329
And so we can see
this request as it

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.400
bounces around the application.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:23.050
We can see two
things in particular

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:24.700
that are probably really bad.

00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:28.377
The first is it goes to
the catalog service a lot.

00:17:28.377 --> 00:17:30.460
So when we're getting
details on a single product,

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:33.040
it doesn't really make sense
that we would continually

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:34.916
call the product catalog
database to find out

00:17:34.916 --> 00:17:36.040
details about that product.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:38.320
That should be cached or,
frankly, it's probably just

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:39.100
poorly written.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:41.392
It is.

00:17:41.392 --> 00:17:43.600
It's poorly written, and
it's just calling it way too

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:45.016
many times instead
of just keeping

00:17:45.016 --> 00:17:47.280
the data in memory somewhere.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.590
And so this sounds like maybe
a somewhat contrived example,

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:54.250
but this is the sort of thing
that we see with our customers.

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:57.895
And certainly I've seen
myself as an engineer, where

00:17:57.895 --> 00:17:59.770
when you don't have
these tools to understand

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:02.103
how your application works,
when you're not tracking how

00:18:02.103 --> 00:18:04.600
these requests
function, you might not

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:05.976
notice that when someone--

00:18:05.976 --> 00:18:07.600
when one engineer
was writing something

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:08.810
and needed more data
from the catalog,

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:10.150
they just made another call.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:11.608
And this adds up
over time, and you

00:18:11.608 --> 00:18:15.580
get these sort of monstrous webs
of application topology in ways

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:17.330
that no one expected
to actually function.

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:19.224
And that's why
you have slowness.

00:18:19.224 --> 00:18:22.146
[AUDIO OUT]

00:18:31.399 --> 00:18:35.127
--even if we had a single call
to the product catalog service,

00:18:35.127 --> 00:18:36.960
most of these are taking
well over a second.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:39.660
This one takes
basically 1.6 seconds.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:42.354
And so the web application
would still be too slow.

00:18:42.354 --> 00:18:44.020
We're going to dig
into the second case.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.270
We already know what the
problem is in the first case.

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:48.840
We know that we need to
reduce the number of calls.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:51.660
But the second case is actually
the harder one to debug,

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.300
where the individual calls
are really, really slow.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.070
And distributed
tracing helps a bit.

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:57.885
We know that the
calls to the catalog

00:18:57.885 --> 00:18:59.160
are causing the slowdown.

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:01.743
That's already taken us through
our first level of triage that

00:19:01.743 --> 00:19:04.310
metrics didn't.

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:06.770
But unless you added a ton of
custom spans to this trace,

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.534
you're not going to
know why it's slow.

00:19:08.534 --> 00:19:09.950
And to understand
that we're going

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:13.410
to go to Stackdriver Debugger--
or sorry, Stackdriver Profiler.

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:16.892
Stackdriver Profiler is a
continuous production profiler.

00:19:16.892 --> 00:19:18.350
What that means is
that it's always

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:21.007
running in the background,
on and off, so to you

00:19:21.007 --> 00:19:23.090
as the developer, it's
effectively always running.

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:24.923
It turns itself on and
off at various points

00:19:24.923 --> 00:19:27.470
to capture profilers--
or capture profiles.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.870
And it has effectively
no performance impact.

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:32.300
That is also true for
Trace and Debugger,

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:34.130
but unlike most
production profilers,

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:35.105
you can leave this one.

00:19:35.105 --> 00:19:37.340
You can effectively just turn
it on, set it, and forget it.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.006
You don't have to
worry about running it

00:19:39.006 --> 00:19:41.420
for 15-minute sessions
or something like that.

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:44.130
You don't have to worry about
it slowing down your app.

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:45.990
Profiler captures
a number of things.

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:47.700
Right now we're looking
at the heap view.

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.158
So we're capturing the memory
consumption of every function

00:19:50.158 --> 00:19:51.600
running on this service.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.190
We can also look
at CPU consumption.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:57.696
We can also look at how
threads are interacting.

00:19:57.696 --> 00:19:59.070
Profiler is great
because it runs

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:01.136
on all of the instances
of your service.

00:20:01.136 --> 00:20:02.760
So just like Trace,
just like Debugger,

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:04.880
you don't have to point it
at a particular instance.

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.004
You don't turn it on a
single instance or some kind

00:20:07.004 --> 00:20:07.887
of canary instance.

00:20:07.887 --> 00:20:09.720
You simply link it to
your code, turn it on,

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.639
and it's running across
all of the instances.

00:20:11.639 --> 00:20:13.680
And you're going to see
performance data captured

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.090
from all of them.

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:16.050
This is the heap view.

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:20.160
We don't see anything
quite too exciting here.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:23.040
We can look for the get product
endpoint, which from the trace

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:25.050
was shown to be the
slow one, or rather the

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.027
get product gRPC
method, which was shown

00:20:27.027 --> 00:20:28.110
to be slow from the trace.

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:30.750
But this is only
consuming 12 and 1/2%

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:32.179
of the application's
total memory.

00:20:32.179 --> 00:20:33.720
And that's still
only about-- what?--

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:35.070
254 kilobytes.

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:38.730
So this is clearly not the
source of our problems.

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:41.871
But when we jump to
CPU consumption--

00:20:41.871 --> 00:20:45.200
just going to take
a swig of my water--

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:46.730
when we jump to
CPU consumption, we

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:48.230
see that things are pretty bad.

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:49.940
So this is the get
product endpoint.

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:52.190
It alone is
consuming roughly 93%

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:56.120
of this application's CPU,
just this one function.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:57.914
And given that
I've played around

00:20:57.914 --> 00:20:59.330
with the catalog
service's code, I

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.163
know that this shouldn't
be the case, right?

00:21:01.163 --> 00:21:03.806
It has a number of
gRPC endpoints--

00:21:03.806 --> 00:21:06.910
or rather gRPC server methods--

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.460
that handle traffic.

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:12.667
It doesn't makes sense that get
product is using so much CPU.

00:21:12.667 --> 00:21:14.500
But when we look down
on this icicle graph--

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.874
and this icicle graph shows
consumption on the horizontal

00:21:16.874 --> 00:21:19.600
axis, and on the vertical
axis, we're showing how these

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.520
functions actually
call each other--

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:25.664
we can see that this calls a
method called Parse Catalog.

00:21:25.664 --> 00:21:28.080
And Parse Catalog is effectively
consuming the same amount

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.500
of CPU as its parent function.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:31.510
So this is where all
the time is spent.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:34.380
And this is where you're both
burning money and burning time.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:37.410
And this seems bad.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:41.460
So next we're actually
going to jump to the code.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.650
This view here is in
Cloud Source Repositories,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:45.150
though the code for
this application

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:46.450
is actually stored on GitHub.

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:48.329
The distinction between
those is kind of

00:21:48.329 --> 00:21:49.370
irrelevant for this demo.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:50.910
But we're actually
jumping to the code,

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:52.951
and we can see we're
basically just deserializing

00:21:52.951 --> 00:21:54.142
a bunch of JSON.

00:21:54.142 --> 00:21:56.350
So what we've already learned
from Trace and Profiler

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:59.070
in a few minutes is that
the catalog is slow,

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.050
or rather requests to
the website are slow.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.330
They're slow because
the catalog is slow.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.550
And the catalog is slow
because this Parse Catalog

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:08.280
method is spending all of
its time deserializing JSON.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.310
And that's probably bad.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:12.872
And so, as a developer,
we've learned something

00:22:12.872 --> 00:22:15.080
that otherwise might be very
difficult to understand,

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:16.621
or we would only
know because we have

00:22:16.621 --> 00:22:18.920
a lot of existing knowledge
of how the system operates.

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:20.503
But we've gone through
this in the way

00:22:20.503 --> 00:22:23.350
that a new developer could go
through it relatively quickly.

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.565
And so Trace and,
in turn, Profiler

00:22:25.565 --> 00:22:27.440
have helped us solve
this performance problem

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:28.315
quickly and reliably.

00:22:28.315 --> 00:22:32.280
And we can apply this to
other parts of the system.

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:34.590
Next I'm going to show
you Stackdriver Debugger.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:36.167
In this case, we
don't have an error

00:22:36.167 --> 00:22:38.500
that we've sort of had time
to code into the application

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:40.125
to debug, so I'm just
going to give you

00:22:40.125 --> 00:22:42.624
a demonstration of debugger
and show you why it's useful.

00:22:42.624 --> 00:22:44.290
So Stackdriver Debugger,
as I mentioned,

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:46.410
is a production debugger.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:49.500
It effectively lets you set
breakpoints in production that

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:51.902
don't halt customer queries.

00:22:51.902 --> 00:22:52.860
This is pretty magical.

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.390
This is one of the things
that when we show it off

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:55.848
at our booth or at
demos like this,

00:22:55.848 --> 00:22:58.020
we get initially a bit of
skepticism from viewers

00:22:58.020 --> 00:22:59.290
saying, "you can't do that.

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:00.750
That's not possible."

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:02.261
But we can.

00:23:02.261 --> 00:23:04.260
And so we present everything
in this really nice

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:05.600
IDE-like interface.

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:07.500
It's not wildly different
than Visual Studio

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.600
Code, or IntelliJ, or others.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:12.840
And you can click in the
gutter and set a thing

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:15.624
that looks like a breakpoint.

00:23:15.624 --> 00:23:17.540
But what we're doing
here is setting something

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:19.280
that we call a snapshot.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.620
And a snapshot is
effectively a breakpoint,

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:23.090
but we don't halt execution.

00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:25.320
So execution flows
right through it.

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:31.080
But we do capture
the local variables.

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:33.939
And we do capture
the stack trace.

00:23:33.939 --> 00:23:35.480
And just like a
traditional debugger,

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.112
you can use conditions
and expressions.

00:23:37.112 --> 00:23:38.820
I'm not going to
demonstrate those today,

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:40.772
but it's got basically
the same functionality

00:23:40.772 --> 00:23:42.980
that you would expect when
you're debugging something

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:45.405
locally on your laptop
or on your desktop.

00:23:45.405 --> 00:23:47.030
And so I'll show that
to you right now.

00:23:47.030 --> 00:23:50.104
We've got a load test running
against this application.

00:23:50.104 --> 00:23:51.770
And so I don't even
have to open the app

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:53.670
to capture data from it.

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:56.450
But if we put a snapshot
right here, for example--

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:58.140
delete that existing one--

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:01.289
we can see how the
code executes inside

00:24:01.289 --> 00:24:02.330
of this convert function.

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:04.970
This convert function is
inside of the currency service.

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:08.320
The currency service will
convert in the storefront.

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:10.980
The storefront lets you
convert from US dollars

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:12.966
to euros to yen,
and that's performed

00:24:12.966 --> 00:24:14.090
by this particular service.

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:14.980
Aaa, where did it go?

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:16.620
There is it.

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:18.480
And so the snapshot
that we laid down

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:20.810
has now been
captured at line 123.

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:23.610
And we can see the
various objects and things

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:24.870
that were captured from it.

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:26.370
And so, for example,
if we wanted

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:28.410
to see the current-- if
there was some kind of issue

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.826
of currency conversion, or we
thought the wrong rates were

00:24:30.826 --> 00:24:32.666
being used, and we were
looking at the input

00:24:32.666 --> 00:24:35.040
and everything seemed right,
we could actually go in here

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:36.690
and jump to the currency
conversion rates that

00:24:36.690 --> 00:24:38.730
are being stored in
memory, in the service,

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:40.740
in production right now.

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:43.020
And so we could very
quickly be able to decide

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:44.340
if the rates that are
being stored are wrong

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:46.506
or if there is some other
kind of problem occurring.

00:24:46.506 --> 00:24:48.630
Similarly, we can look at
any of the objects here.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.010
We can also jump
through the call stack

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.870
and capture the local
variables captured

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:53.490
in each of those frames.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:55.230
So it's just the
exact same power

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:57.210
you would expect from
a local debugger,

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:59.460
except it's running
live in production

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:01.840
and will not affect anything.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:03.174
So it's really, really powerful.

00:25:03.174 --> 00:25:04.965
We use the same thing
at Google with a tool

00:25:04.965 --> 00:25:06.930
called Borg Debugger
that's basically the same.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:09.900
It's really, really nice for
solving rare production issues.

00:25:18.489 --> 00:25:20.530
So I'm just going to sort
of double click on some

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:22.180
of the key benefits that we saw.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:24.397
So Stackdriver Debugger
snapshots, as well as

00:25:24.397 --> 00:25:26.230
a feature in Debugger
called log points that

00:25:26.230 --> 00:25:29.680
allows you to push out log
statements to your application

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:32.110
instantly without
redeploying it,

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:35.820
remove a ton of deployment
steps doing root cause analysis.

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:37.990
Today, if you're
developing on Kubernetes

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:39.682
or some kind of
distributed system,

00:25:39.682 --> 00:25:41.890
and you're often in the
production environment, where

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:43.750
it's either difficult
or just impossible

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:47.785
to attach a debugger, you would
often solve problems like this

00:25:47.785 --> 00:25:49.660
by adding log
statements to your code.

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:50.680
Whenever you have
some kind of error,

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:51.730
you add a bunch
of log statements

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:53.830
to your code to flush
out how it's operating

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:56.659
and add things to logs, redeploy
your app with the error still

00:25:56.659 --> 00:25:59.200
in tow because you don't know
what the actual error is caused

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:00.340
by.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:01.349
That can take a while.

00:26:01.349 --> 00:26:03.640
Then you get to read the
logs, using your logging tool,

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:06.139
and find out if the system is
functioning the way you expect

00:26:06.139 --> 00:26:08.599
or maybe get some insight
into what the error is.

00:26:08.599 --> 00:26:09.390
Maybe you found it.

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:10.860
Maybe you need to add
more log statements.

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:12.020
So you go through
typically a couple

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:13.420
of iteration cycles
of deploying things

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:15.540
to production, which is
painful and slow and bad

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:16.940
for your customers.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:18.399
With snapshots and
then log points,

00:26:18.399 --> 00:26:20.773
which allow you to just added
those log statements if you

00:26:20.773 --> 00:26:23.130
want the firehose view, you
can do that [SNAPS FINGERS]

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:25.050
instantly, which is
really, really powerful.

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:27.700
And this is why we developed
Debugger inside of Google.

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:29.575
And this is why we use
it so often, including

00:26:29.575 --> 00:26:31.077
some of our biggest names.

00:26:31.077 --> 00:26:32.535
Next we had a
Stackdriver Profiler.

00:26:32.535 --> 00:26:35.060
So Profiler operates
at extreme scale.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:36.930
But unlike most
profilers, and certainly

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:38.630
unlike most
production profilers,

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:41.420
has effectively no
performance impact.

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:43.830
It's also not limited to
analyzing single transactions.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:46.610
It gives you that
continuous flame graph

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:49.380
view for how your application
has been running in production.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:51.290
So it gives you great
insight into the amount

00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:53.754
of CPU used by each
function in your code

00:26:53.754 --> 00:26:55.170
as well as the
amount of heap use.

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:57.086
And you can also look
at threads in contention

00:26:57.086 --> 00:26:58.230
and other things like that.

00:26:58.230 --> 00:26:59.855
And so it gives you
a lot of visibility

00:26:59.855 --> 00:27:02.320
into how your core code is
running that you wouldn't have

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:04.520
without a tool like Profiler.

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:07.030
In particular, the heap view
is really useful for detecting

00:27:07.030 --> 00:27:08.825
things like slow memory leaks.

00:27:08.825 --> 00:27:10.200
Profiler, in
addition to give you

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.074
that understanding of
how your code operates,

00:27:12.074 --> 00:27:14.420
is a powerful
cost-reduction tool.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:16.510
So in the private
alpha for Profiler,

00:27:16.510 --> 00:27:19.330
we had a number of customers
come back to us and say,

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:20.740
with about one
hour of time, they

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:24.294
shave 10% off their CPU
bill, off their compute bill.

00:27:24.294 --> 00:27:25.960
Depending on your
scale, that might just

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:27.250
be a small amount of
money and something

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:28.900
that doesn't really
interest you.

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:31.270
But once you start
running a lot of nodes

00:27:31.270 --> 00:27:33.310
and your monthly compute
bill is fairly high,

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:34.990
this is really
tangible money that you

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:37.370
can save with a very
small amount of time.

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:38.590
Additionally-- and I'll
get into this in a minute--

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:40.780
we had some larger customers
who were fighting performance

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:42.370
problems with some
of their services

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:44.320
that they were having to scale
way beyond what they thought

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:45.190
was needed.

00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:46.900
And with Profiler,
they were able to get

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:51.040
back to their expected scale
by finding, in particular,

00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:55.120
areas of high CPU consumption
that they didn't expect.

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:57.240
Finally I showed you
Stackdriver Trace.

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:00.577
Tracing is finally becoming a
much more popular and much more

00:28:00.577 --> 00:28:02.910
well understood thing amongst
the development community,

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:04.242
which is awesome.

00:28:04.242 --> 00:28:05.700
But tracing tools
tend to either be

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:08.310
expensive or difficult
to adopt these days.

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:09.940
They are certainly
open source options.

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:11.727
Zipkin and Jaeger are great.

00:28:11.727 --> 00:28:13.560
But you have to go and
manage them yourself,

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:15.277
and spin up the Ms,
and certainly manage

00:28:15.277 --> 00:28:17.610
the storage for them, which
can be annoying or sometimes

00:28:17.610 --> 00:28:19.142
expensive.

00:28:19.142 --> 00:28:20.850
There are also paid
options in the space.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:23.100
They also tend to be
relatively pricey.

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:25.410
Stackdriver Trace is
neither of those things.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:27.790
It is simple, easy to get
started with, and incredibly

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:28.290
cheap.

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:33.730
So I mentioned a
Profiler success story.

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:37.210
So Snapchat-- you know, it
wouldn't be talk at NEXT

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:38.916
without mentioning Snapchat.

00:28:38.916 --> 00:28:40.540
Snapchat was one of
the alpha customers

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:43.780
for Stackdriver Profiler along
with a couple of other marquee

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:45.790
customers.

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:47.980
They used it to find
some performance issues

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:51.460
that they hadn't been able
to discover previously.

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:53.410
They left us this
very nice quote

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:59.740
about it being able to pinpoint
big consumers of CPU time,

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:01.810
and at snapscale,
certainly very large.

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:04.330
They were able to both
reduce their spend

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:05.830
on a number of
services and increase

00:29:05.830 --> 00:29:06.913
their overall performance.

00:29:06.913 --> 00:29:08.971
So for them it was
incredibly powerful.

00:29:08.971 --> 00:29:11.470
We had similar stories come
from a number of our other alpha

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:12.962
clients as well as.

00:29:12.962 --> 00:29:15.170
Certainly now that Profiler
has going to public beta,

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:16.003
we've had even more.

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:20.230
So that wraps up our
tour, our whirlwind tour

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:22.500
of Stackdriver's APM tools.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:25.560
I didn't do a demo of things
like Stackdriver Monitoring

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:28.252
or Stackdriver's new
Kubernetes monitoring tools.

00:29:28.252 --> 00:29:30.460
There are a number of other
talks happening on those.

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:32.610
But what I really
wanted to show is

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:36.330
how these same workflows that
we have at Google to both reduce

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:38.940
latency for applications
as well as find

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:42.600
the cause of errors,
how you can apply those

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.995
at your own companies.

00:29:44.995 --> 00:29:46.620
Over the years and
in the coming years,

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:49.110
we're going to have even
more launching in this space.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:50.850
But even today,
we've externalized

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:52.600
enough of our
application tooling,

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:54.660
or enough of our
developer tooling,

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.420
that external developers
can effectively

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:01.084
go through the same workflows
as us, which is awesome.

00:30:01.084 --> 00:30:03.440
There was one last thing
I wanted to touch on.

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:06.770
So Stackdriver's APM
tools have no limitations

00:30:06.770 --> 00:30:08.310
on where they can be used.

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:10.430
So if you have hybrid
cloud applications, things

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:12.800
that are running on premises,
things that are running

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:15.050
on your laptop even,
there's no restriction

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:17.510
on where the applications
that are being instrumented,

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:18.700
where they're running.

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:23.470
And so there's no requirement to
only use this on GCP resources.

00:30:23.470 --> 00:30:25.280
And that's even with
the pricing plans.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.155
So Stackdriver Profiler,
Stackdriver Debugger

00:30:27.155 --> 00:30:28.250
are currently free.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:30.660
Stackdriver Trace has a
pretty generous free tier,

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:32.150
and then is a paid tier
above that that's coming

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:33.210
into effect in September.

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:35.630
But this is all the
same, regardless of where

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:37.140
your applications are running.

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:39.140
And we think it's a really
competitive offering.

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:41.432
If you have stuff that's
running just on another cloud,

00:30:41.432 --> 00:30:43.056
we would love for
you to use our tools.

00:30:43.056 --> 00:30:44.130
I would be awesome.

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:46.310
And we think you'll get
a lot of value out of it.

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.070
So I promised this talk would
be divided into two parts.

00:30:49.070 --> 00:30:51.350
The second part is
about OpenCensus.

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:53.360
So to give you a bit
of an introduction,

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:56.660
OpenCensus is an
instrumentation project

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:58.490
that we undertook
roughly about a year

00:30:58.490 --> 00:31:01.050
and a half ago,
maybe two years ago.

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:04.050
And it's basically
we've been trying

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:07.200
to work with other vendors so
that we can get better tracing

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:10.860
and metrics instrumentation for
our tools and for their tools.

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:13.320
For those who aren't as familiar
with distributed tracing,

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:15.111
one of the biggest
challenges of the space,

00:31:15.111 --> 00:31:17.460
for vendors, for developers,
for everyone else,

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:19.930
is getting traces to come out
of their apps automatically.

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:22.070
So I mentioned before that
at Google, with Dapper,

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:22.986
we're able to do this.

00:31:22.986 --> 00:31:24.770
And it's really powerful.

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:27.420
It helps us understand how our
apps are distributed as well

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:29.034
as debug problems.

00:31:29.034 --> 00:31:30.700
But we can do that
because we're running

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:31.990
a custom version of the JVM.

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:33.310
We have our own RPC library.

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:36.760
And so we do a lot of things
under the hood to enable this

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:38.410
that external developers can't.

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:41.230
Most shops aren't going to
go modify the JVM extensively

00:31:41.230 --> 00:31:42.950
to go do things that they want.

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:45.880
They're not going to go
build their own RPC system.

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:47.640
And so with OpenCensus,
we're trying

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:50.040
to make this ubiquitous to
developers outside the company

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:52.410
as it is to Googlers.

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:54.840
And to do this we
need to go and build

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:57.120
integrations of every single
thing that you have that

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:58.800
sends and receives
an RPC, or anything

00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:01.320
that really does a
slow-running transaction.

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:03.600
So where we and other
vendors have struggled

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:05.970
in the past has been that you
need to build integrations

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:10.590
across every language for HTTP,
other RPC libraries, storage

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:14.250
client libraries,
things for some kind

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:18.270
of slow-running process that
runs on each machine for things

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:21.240
like Kafka, for basically
any sort of client library

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:24.540
or anything you interact
with from tens or hundreds

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:25.470
of vendors.

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:28.110
And it's really slow and
really painful to build these.

00:32:28.110 --> 00:32:30.960
And so we thought,
"well that's silly."

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.447
So with OpenCensus,
we've gone together--

00:32:33.447 --> 00:32:35.280
and I'll have a list
of vendors in a minute.

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.100
But we've actually partnered up
with a number of other vendors

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.325
in this space to go
build those integrations,

00:32:40.325 --> 00:32:42.450
so you can automatically
get traces and metrics out

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:45.580
of your app, and then do some
basic analysis on top of those.

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:47.047
And so it makes
it, as a developer,

00:32:47.047 --> 00:32:49.380
it makes it really easy to
get started with these tools,

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:52.399
whether that's with Stackdriver
APM, or Stackdriver Monitoring,

00:32:52.399 --> 00:32:53.940
or with other
solutions like Datadog,

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:56.600
or SolarWinds, or a number
of our other partners.

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:00.956
So back to the
demo app, and we're

00:33:00.956 --> 00:33:02.580
going to do another
demo of OpenCensus.

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:04.579
But this is sort of--

00:33:04.579 --> 00:33:06.120
there's no lines
connecting anything,

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:08.460
but this is roughly the
structure of that Hipster Shop

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:09.390
demo.

00:33:09.390 --> 00:33:11.790
So it's got a frontend
service, email service,

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:13.170
currency service, and others.

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:15.211
And as I mentioned before,
they're written in Go,

00:33:15.211 --> 00:33:17.890
Python Node.js and C#.

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:19.140
I listed a database here.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.570
The demo that we're using
just uses an in-memory data--

00:33:21.570 --> 00:33:23.278
like just basically
just has a big object

00:33:23.278 --> 00:33:24.732
in memory as its database.

00:33:24.732 --> 00:33:27.190
But they Hipster Shop demo,
once it's published externally,

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:28.980
I think it's going to connect
to some kind of SQL database.

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:31.230
And so just like any other
customer application,

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:34.050
you have to have integrations
for all these things, right?

00:33:34.050 --> 00:33:35.820
Even if you're
using HTTP and gRPC,

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:37.710
you need to go build
integrations for HTTP

00:33:37.710 --> 00:33:40.635
and gRPC across every language.

00:33:40.635 --> 00:33:42.412
If that database
there is MongoDB

00:33:42.412 --> 00:33:44.370
or some kind of SQL
database or something else,

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:46.770
in order to get traces and
metrics from interactions

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:48.960
with it, we need to go
build those integrations.

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:51.750
This is, again, just the problem
that OpenCensus is a solving.

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:54.150
I'm sure a number of you
from larger, older companies

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:56.370
can attest to,
you'll have services

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.620
running in probably
five plus languages,

00:33:58.620 --> 00:34:01.770
using 10 to 20 different
storage technologies that

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:04.050
communicate to each other
using various different RPC

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:05.220
frameworks.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:07.981
The real world out
there is a mess.

00:34:07.981 --> 00:34:10.230
Software is developed over
a very long period of time.

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:11.550
Technologies change.

00:34:11.550 --> 00:34:13.690
Old code doesn't
necessarily get deleted.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:16.020
And so in order to make
all these things automatic,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:17.880
we need to do a lot
of work to actually

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:22.320
make traces and metric show up.

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:25.110
So to come back to
what OpenCensus is,

00:34:25.110 --> 00:34:26.969
OpenCensus is a
collection of libraries

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:28.650
that we publish on
a per-language basis

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:31.080
to collect traces and metrics.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:32.550
We have support for--

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.300
and I'll get to the
full list in a minute,

00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:38.400
but we're targeting support
for Java, Go Node, .NET, Ruby,

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:41.940
Python, PHP and C++.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:44.340
So it's a list of
eight languages.

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:47.620
The OpenCensus libraries
for each language

00:34:47.620 --> 00:34:50.050
have sort of three
layers to them.

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:51.639
They have the
instrumentation layer,

00:34:51.639 --> 00:34:53.764
so that's the things that
I was discussing earlier,

00:34:53.764 --> 00:34:56.197
the automatic plug-ins for
various storage frameworks,

00:34:56.197 --> 00:34:57.780
the RPC frameworks,
basically anything

00:34:57.780 --> 00:34:59.659
you want to capture a
trace or metric from.

00:34:59.659 --> 00:35:00.450
They have the APIs.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:02.783
So as a developer, you can
manually interact with those.

00:35:02.783 --> 00:35:04.740
You can create your
own custom metrics.

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:06.270
You can change the
metrics that are

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:07.680
being automatically collected.

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:09.480
You can add
annotations to traces.

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:12.430
You can change the trace
sampling policy and so on.

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:13.860
And finally, the
reason that we've

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:17.130
been working with other vendors
is this concept of exporters.

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:19.409
So exporters are these really
small lightweight pieces

00:35:19.409 --> 00:35:21.450
of code that allow you to
send the data collected

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:23.700
by OpenCensus to your
backend of choice.

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:25.530
One of those backends
is Stackdriver.

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:28.290
One of those backends is just
writing everything out to logs.

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:29.790
But you can also
send it to backends

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:33.720
like Datadog and SolarWinds
and Instana and a number

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:35.490
of other vendors in this space.

00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:37.949
And the nice thing
about OpenCensus

00:35:37.949 --> 00:35:40.240
is that the exporters are
really small amounts of code.

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:42.870
Like if you wanted to
support some new backend,

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:45.060
you don't have to go
reimplement the tracer

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:47.289
and reimplement how
sampling works or context

00:35:47.289 --> 00:35:49.830
propagation for tracing, like
any of these really complicated

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:50.465
things.

00:35:50.465 --> 00:35:51.840
You just write a
bit of code that

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:54.210
translates the data that
OpenCensus has in memory

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:57.180
to the format you expect and
send and RPC to your backend.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:58.572
So that's really, really simple.

00:35:58.572 --> 00:36:00.780
And our goal with OpenCensus
right from the beginning

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:02.863
is to be able to include
as much functionality out

00:36:02.863 --> 00:36:04.719
of the box as possible.

00:36:04.719 --> 00:36:06.510
The scenario we want
for most developers is

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:09.440
you link an OpenCensus library
to your app, traces and metrics

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:09.940
come out.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:10.440
That's it.

00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:12.814
You choose an exporter , then
traces and metrics come out

00:36:12.814 --> 00:36:14.030
and get sent to your backend.

00:36:14.030 --> 00:36:16.530
We don't want developers to be
sitting there plumbing things

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:18.090
and gluing things together.

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:18.965
It's really painful.

00:36:18.965 --> 00:36:21.090
It means that they're not
going to use those tools.

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:22.680
And quite frankly,
as a company that's

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:23.940
in the business of
selling more and more

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:26.100
distributed systems through
technologies like Kubernetes,

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:27.820
we want to make this
as easy as possible.

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:31.690
That's why we invest
in OpenCensus.

00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:33.310
So as I mentioned
before, we have

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:35.393
support for a number of
languages and integrations

00:36:35.393 --> 00:36:36.400
and exporter.

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:40.570
Right now we have really great
support for Java, C++ and Go.

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:42.970
For Ruby, Python,
and PHP, the tracing

00:36:42.970 --> 00:36:44.600
is in a production-ready state.

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:47.440
And we've been adding
support for metrics.

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:49.900
Some of those are a little
further along than others.

00:36:49.900 --> 00:36:53.271
And Node.js I think
just entered in alpha,

00:36:53.271 --> 00:36:54.270
if I remember correctly.

00:36:54.270 --> 00:36:56.530
And so Node.js is going
to jump to full support

00:36:56.530 --> 00:36:59.360
pretty quickly,
as well as Python.

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:01.780
.NET is under development
through our partnership with

00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:02.290
Microsoft.

00:37:02.290 --> 00:37:03.682
I'll get to that in a minute.

00:37:03.682 --> 00:37:05.890
But they're going to be
handling development of .NET,

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:08.014
and I think they also have
a pretty strong interest

00:37:08.014 --> 00:37:10.710
in Python and Node.

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:12.360
We have a huge number
of integrations,

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:14.943
far too many for me to list, and
certainly far too many for me

00:37:14.943 --> 00:37:17.900
to have thrown into this
presentation at last minute.

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:24.135
But in general, we support
HTTP, gRPC, SQL, SQL clients,

00:37:24.135 --> 00:37:27.300
MongoDB, most things that
you would expect to be there.

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:29.610
If you're using OpenCensus
and something isn't there

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:31.880
that you expect, you
can reach out to us.

00:37:31.880 --> 00:37:34.440
Certainly we would love if
you made a pull request.

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:37.709
The whole goal of this project
is to get as many integrations

00:37:37.709 --> 00:37:39.750
right into the main client
library so that people

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.780
can use them out of the box and
so that multiple vendors can

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.990
rely on and maintain the
same set of integrations.

00:37:45.990 --> 00:37:48.180
Finally we have
exporters for a number

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.404
of really popular backends.

00:37:50.404 --> 00:37:52.320
We've targeted our
functionality on OpenCensus

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:55.500
to always work for our two
reference backends for tracing

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:56.020
and metrics.

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:58.110
So Zipkin and Prometheus
support is always

00:37:58.110 --> 00:37:59.550
there and always guaranteed.

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:02.890
We also support Jaeger,
SignalFx, Datadog, SolarWinds,

00:38:02.890 --> 00:38:06.330
Instana, Amazon X-Ray,
and of course Stackdriver.

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:09.260
More and more backends are
being added all the time.

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:11.257
With the current
architecture of OpenCensus,

00:38:11.257 --> 00:38:12.840
these exporters have
to be implemented

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:14.294
on a per-language basis.

00:38:14.294 --> 00:38:16.710
And so some languages might
have more support while others

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:17.221
don't.

00:38:17.221 --> 00:38:18.720
At the end of the
presentation, I'll

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:20.670
talk about a new
project we're working

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:24.937
on that's going to expand
that support even further.

00:38:24.937 --> 00:38:26.770
OpenCensus also includes
some neat features.

00:38:26.770 --> 00:38:29.020
So I mentioned analysis earlier.

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:31.150
So while it's mostly an
instrumentation project,

00:38:31.150 --> 00:38:34.090
OpenCensus also gives
you some very quick ways

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:35.840
to understand what's
going on in your app.

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:42.240
The most prominent of those
is something we call Z-Pages.

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:46.100
So if you read "Hacker
News" a lot like I do,

00:38:46.100 --> 00:38:49.730
there was a post one or two
days ago about debugging

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:52.370
and how we-- sort of some
of the Borg debugging

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:53.999
features used at Google.

00:38:53.999 --> 00:38:55.790
One of the things that
was mentioned there,

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:57.920
and all the top-ranked
comments talked about

00:38:57.920 --> 00:39:01.940
was these sort of Borg
status pages or Z-Pages.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:04.460
And we provide these
through OpenCensus

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:06.650
to developers around the world.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:10.160
These Z-Pages are basically
small web pages hosted

00:39:10.160 --> 00:39:12.740
by the OpenCensus client
library on each node

00:39:12.740 --> 00:39:13.940
of your application.

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:18.290
It shows basic
summaries of any RPCs

00:39:18.290 --> 00:39:20.090
that have been going
through the system

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:22.340
as well as summaries of all
the stats on your system.

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:24.944
So it basically gives you
this little firehose view

00:39:24.944 --> 00:39:27.110
for the instance of your
app that you're inspecting.

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:29.300
It's not necessarily as
useful as looking at something

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:31.520
like a distributed tracing
backend, like Stackdriver

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:33.490
Trace, which shows you
the end-to-end view.

00:39:33.490 --> 00:39:35.940
These only show data
captured from a single node.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:37.315
But if you think
that something's

00:39:37.315 --> 00:39:39.770
wrong with a particular node,
or if your debugging process

00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:41.390
starts with inspecting
a single node,

00:39:41.390 --> 00:39:45.069
these Z-Pages are a great
place to go find that data.

00:39:45.069 --> 00:39:46.610
One of the other
interesting features

00:39:46.610 --> 00:39:50.480
we have is our work on the
W3C Trace-Context format.

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:53.570
So about a year and a half
ago, Google and Zipkin

00:39:53.570 --> 00:39:57.170
started work on this
shared context propagation

00:39:57.170 --> 00:39:59.207
format for distributed traces.

00:39:59.207 --> 00:40:01.040
If you're familiar with
distributed tracing,

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:02.498
you might already
be aware of this.

00:40:02.498 --> 00:40:04.486
But one of the
challenges in the space

00:40:04.486 --> 00:40:06.360
is if you're using
different client libraries

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:09.200
or different
instrumentation frameworks,

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:12.240
traces still won't
always work together.

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:14.220
And so take an example of HTTP.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:16.730
If I have service A,
service B, and service C,

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:18.746
in order to get a
distributed trace,

00:40:18.746 --> 00:40:20.120
my distributed
tracing system has

00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:22.290
to know that a single
request is correlated.

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:26.760
And so Zipkin uses, for example,
the B3 headers that basically

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:29.300
set a trace ID for this trace.

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:31.164
We started this
project with Zipkin,

00:40:31.164 --> 00:40:32.580
and now a number
of other vendors,

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:36.500
including Microsoft and others,
to basically it will snap,

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:39.560
align everybody onto this same
context propagation format.

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:41.909
If you're using a
single-vendor system,

00:40:41.909 --> 00:40:43.700
this probably doesn't
interest you as much.

00:40:43.700 --> 00:40:45.860
But it's another way that
we're able to work better

00:40:45.860 --> 00:40:46.651
with the community.

00:40:46.651 --> 00:40:47.972
And it's really nice.

00:40:47.972 --> 00:40:49.430
Finally, a neat
thing of OpenCensus

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:52.410
is just that you can use
multiple exporters at once.

00:40:52.410 --> 00:40:55.340
So you can send traces and
metrics to as many backends

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:57.200
As you'd like.

00:40:57.200 --> 00:40:58.700
For example, we
might have customers

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:01.700
who do some metrics
to Prometheus

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:04.850
and some go to Stackdriver,
or rather all go to both.

00:41:04.850 --> 00:41:06.892
But if you're
either transitioning

00:41:06.892 --> 00:41:08.600
between using different
vendors or you're

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:10.160
at some kind of
mixed-mode environment,

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:12.243
where different teams use
different systems, which

00:41:12.243 --> 00:41:15.980
is actually relatively common,
OpenCensus will support that.

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:17.764
So if some company
gets acquired, comes

00:41:17.764 --> 00:41:20.180
into your company, they're
using a different set of tools.

00:41:20.180 --> 00:41:21.530
As long as you're
using OpenCensus,

00:41:21.530 --> 00:41:23.330
you can send a telemetry
to all of those tools

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:25.205
and satisfy everybody,
which is really great.

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:31.320
All right so, once again, I'm
going to hop over to my demo.

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:35.650
So inside of this
application, we've

00:41:35.650 --> 00:41:38.280
gone and already added
OpenCensus to the services.

00:41:38.280 --> 00:41:41.230
So I'm going to pull up the
code for the product catalog

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:41.730
service.

00:41:44.990 --> 00:41:48.370
There you are.

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:51.795
And so we're using OpenCensus
in the product catalog service

00:41:51.795 --> 00:41:53.420
in order to capture
traces and metrics.

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:00.310
And most of the code
is captured here.

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:04.720
Just like I described, this
is a really nice scenario,

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:07.090
where you just basically
initialize the OpenCensus

00:42:07.090 --> 00:42:10.750
service, choose an exporter,
and it captures effectively

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:12.510
everything you
want automatically.

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:16.540
In this init tracing function,
we register OpenCensus,

00:42:16.540 --> 00:42:18.532
we tell it to use the
Stackdriver Exporter.

00:42:18.532 --> 00:42:20.740
And we tell it to do that
for both tracing and stats,

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:21.573
and that's about it.

00:42:21.573 --> 00:42:24.130
I think we also might mention
that we're using gRPC.

00:42:24.130 --> 00:42:26.530
Beyond that, OpenCensus
will automatically go

00:42:26.530 --> 00:42:27.801
and instrument gRPC.

00:42:27.801 --> 00:42:29.800
It's going to capture
application-level metrics,

00:42:29.800 --> 00:42:33.340
so things like latency
and packets-- sorry,

00:42:33.340 --> 00:42:34.780
not packet size--

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:36.586
request size and queue
length and others,

00:42:36.586 --> 00:42:37.960
and it's also
automatically going

00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:40.780
to capture distributed
traces and propagate context.

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:44.240
And we saw that in the
Stackdriver Trace view.

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:47.230
And now I'm going to go try and
open the Metrics view to show

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:48.230
you the example of that.

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:50.314
Here we go.

00:42:50.314 --> 00:42:52.220
I'm just going to
zoom out if I can.

00:42:52.220 --> 00:42:54.750
So these are the metrics
captured by OpenCensus.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:58.330
This is inside of Stackdriver.

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:01.720
This is not working
as I want it to.

00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:04.840
And it's going to capture--
it's showing us the latency

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:06.270
of our application--

00:43:08.910 --> 00:43:10.767
here we go-- the server latency.

00:43:10.767 --> 00:43:13.350
And we're going to group this
by the different server methods.

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:16.220
So we can see the latency
across each server method.

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:18.970
And let's look at
the 95th percentile.

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:19.470
There we go.

00:43:19.470 --> 00:43:20.386
Oh, I see the problem.

00:43:20.386 --> 00:43:23.110
My screen is just
too zoomed to--

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:25.240
anyways, I can click on these.

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:27.250
If the screen was
higher resolution,

00:43:27.250 --> 00:43:30.040
you would see the various
latencies, 95th percentile

00:43:30.040 --> 00:43:33.477
latency, for each of the
different services contained

00:43:33.477 --> 00:43:34.310
in this application.

00:43:34.310 --> 00:43:37.372
So it's just with five
or six lines of code,

00:43:37.372 --> 00:43:38.830
I'm now able to
get latency metrics

00:43:38.830 --> 00:43:43.090
from all the different methods
handled on this single service.

00:43:43.090 --> 00:43:46.810
Once I roll this out across all
the services in my application,

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:49.630
I now get to
understand the latency,

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:52.790
latency characteristics, of
every single endpoint attached

00:43:52.790 --> 00:43:55.090
to every single service,
which is really, really nice.

00:43:55.090 --> 00:43:58.040
And of course, we
already saw the traces.

00:43:58.040 --> 00:44:00.865
But I also mentioned that
OpenCensus does custom metrics.

00:44:00.865 --> 00:44:02.740
And so in addition to
automatically capturing

00:44:02.740 --> 00:44:04.750
these metrics,
you as a developer

00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:06.310
can go and add your own.

00:44:06.310 --> 00:44:09.720
So what we had
added here, I think

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:16.100
to the cart service or
the checkout service,

00:44:16.100 --> 00:44:23.930
was the ability to see how
many checkout requests are

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:24.970
going through the app.

00:44:24.970 --> 00:44:26.992
And so I've done that here.

00:44:26.992 --> 00:44:27.984
I'll just pull it up.

00:44:27.984 --> 00:44:29.472
Apologies for the delay.

00:44:37.408 --> 00:44:40.880
OpenCensus-- here we go.

00:44:45.514 --> 00:44:47.680
And so in the code, which
I'll show you in a minute,

00:44:47.680 --> 00:44:50.590
we're capturing the
number of items in a cart

00:44:50.590 --> 00:44:51.880
when the cart is checked out.

00:44:51.880 --> 00:44:53.470
And we can see the results here.

00:44:53.470 --> 00:44:57.215
And the code for this
was pretty simple.

00:44:57.215 --> 00:44:59.610
Where on earth is it?

00:44:59.610 --> 00:45:01.047
Checkout service, there we go.

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:08.430
So we've registered
the view up above.

00:45:08.430 --> 00:45:10.830
This is how we create
our new metric.

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:13.080
And then all we have to do
is scroll down to the code,

00:45:13.080 --> 00:45:15.750
where we actually capture
it, which is a single line.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:17.104
Here it is.

00:45:17.104 --> 00:45:18.520
And with this
single line of code,

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:20.200
in addition to few
register the view,

00:45:20.200 --> 00:45:21.340
I've been able to
capture a custom

00:45:21.340 --> 00:45:22.550
metric for my application.

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:26.037
And I can send it to the backend
of my choice, which is great.

00:45:26.037 --> 00:45:27.370
You can send this to Prometheus.

00:45:27.370 --> 00:45:28.600
You can send this
to Stackdriver.

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:30.697
You can send this to
Datadog, wherever you really

00:45:30.697 --> 00:45:31.280
want it to go.

00:45:31.280 --> 00:45:32.436
And it's not much work.

00:45:32.436 --> 00:45:34.060
It's both a great
developer experience.

00:45:34.060 --> 00:45:35.680
If you're using
a single backend,

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:38.270
you can roll this out across
your entire application.

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:40.120
But it's a great developer--
it's an even better developer

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:42.036
experience if you're
working multiple backends

00:45:42.036 --> 00:45:43.890
in a mixed-mode environment.

00:45:43.890 --> 00:45:46.450
And so, like I mentioned
before, our goal of OpenCensus

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:48.960
is to make as much of this
automatic-- as automatic and as

00:45:48.960 --> 00:45:49.990
easy as possible.

00:45:49.990 --> 00:45:51.948
And I think with this
demo we've achieved that.

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:57.440
So I mentioned some really
interesting features

00:45:57.440 --> 00:45:58.998
that are coming to OpenCensus.

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:03.190
The first is just
more integrations.

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:06.400
So today we've got a pretty big
list that we're quite proud of.

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:08.500
But as time goes on, we
want to add the ability

00:46:08.500 --> 00:46:11.800
to get more traces and metrics
automatically from more

00:46:11.800 --> 00:46:13.760
and more different stacks.

00:46:13.760 --> 00:46:15.260
So, for example,
like today, I don't

00:46:15.260 --> 00:46:16.981
think we support Google
Pub/Sub or Kafka.

00:46:16.981 --> 00:46:18.230
We're going to be adding that.

00:46:18.230 --> 00:46:20.521
We're going to be adding it
for as many technologies as

00:46:20.521 --> 00:46:21.074
possible.

00:46:21.074 --> 00:46:22.490
The next that's
really interesting

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:24.530
is this thing we call
the OpenCensus collector

00:46:24.530 --> 00:46:26.262
service and smarter sampling.

00:46:26.262 --> 00:46:28.220
So one of the challenges
of distributed tracing

00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:31.004
today is that requests
come into your application,

00:46:31.004 --> 00:46:32.420
they get traced,
and typically you

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:35.269
sample those that like a 1%
rate or something like that.

00:46:35.269 --> 00:46:37.310
The problem with this is
that the average request

00:46:37.310 --> 00:46:38.524
isn't very interesting.

00:46:38.524 --> 00:46:40.190
If you think of the
average request that

00:46:40.190 --> 00:46:42.620
goes to an e-commerce
backend or even to Google,

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:44.240
it's probably boring.

00:46:44.240 --> 00:46:45.780
It sits around the
50th percentile.

00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:47.280
If you're trying
to analyze latency,

00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:48.770
it's not especially useful.

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:51.020
Typically you want to look
at requests that are either

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:53.660
correlated with errors or
requests that are slow,

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:56.889
in your 95th percentile latency,
99th percentile latency.

00:46:56.889 --> 00:46:58.430
This is, of course,
very challenging,

00:46:58.430 --> 00:47:00.740
because today when you make a
sampling decision on a request,

00:47:00.740 --> 00:47:01.860
you usually do that
when it arrives.

00:47:01.860 --> 00:47:03.380
And whatever request
comes in, you

00:47:03.380 --> 00:47:05.713
don't really have a great way
of predicting whether it's

00:47:05.713 --> 00:47:07.400
going to be slow or not.

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:09.290
What we're doing--
what we're trying to do

00:47:09.290 --> 00:47:13.010
is build this service you can
deploy within your application.

00:47:13.010 --> 00:47:14.750
Traces and metrics
from all of your nodes

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:16.291
are sent to that
service, and then we

00:47:16.291 --> 00:47:18.240
can make the sampling
decision later.

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:20.615
And so the sampling decision
will be made on the service,

00:47:20.615 --> 00:47:22.950
and then the traces will
be sent to your backend.

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:25.100
And so, for example,
you could configure it

00:47:25.100 --> 00:47:28.180
to sample traces that have
something like 99th percentile

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:30.410
latency, 95th
percentile latency,

00:47:30.410 --> 00:47:33.162
traces for requests that
are relatively uncommon,

00:47:33.162 --> 00:47:35.120
basically traces that
are far more interesting.

00:47:35.120 --> 00:47:36.494
As an application
developer, this

00:47:36.494 --> 00:47:38.720
means you're actually getting
actionable data instead

00:47:38.720 --> 00:47:40.303
of spending all of
your time and money

00:47:40.303 --> 00:47:42.950
on collecting traces that
are, frankly, not that useful.

00:47:42.950 --> 00:47:44.630
And so this is like
a big, big thing

00:47:44.630 --> 00:47:47.580
that OpenCensus is
going to provide soon.

00:47:47.580 --> 00:47:49.170
Next, after that,
is providing support

00:47:49.170 --> 00:47:50.680
for client applications.

00:47:50.680 --> 00:47:52.890
So one of the challenges
of tracing solutions today,

00:47:52.890 --> 00:47:55.560
or at least some of them,
including Stackdriver,

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:57.425
is that they don't support--

00:47:57.425 --> 00:47:59.550
they don't really give you
true end-to-end latency.

00:47:59.550 --> 00:48:02.190
You can see the latency of an
operation that occurs inside

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:04.950
of your web application or
inside of your data center

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:06.870
or inside of Google
Cloud, for example.

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:08.670
But typically you're
not getting visibility

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:10.170
into the actual
latency perceived

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:13.680
by a client, whether that client
is a frontend web application

00:48:13.680 --> 00:48:15.595
or that client is like
an Android or iOS app.

00:48:15.595 --> 00:48:17.220
And so we're going
to be adding support

00:48:17.220 --> 00:48:18.219
for those in OpenCensus.

00:48:18.219 --> 00:48:21.540
So we can capture traces,
all the way from your user's

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:24.090
interaction with some
of their software,

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:26.880
all the way to going to
your back and then back.

00:48:26.880 --> 00:48:29.160
And so instead of seeing
that a user interaction took

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:31.380
200 milliseconds and wondering
why your customers say

00:48:31.380 --> 00:48:33.480
that your app is
slow, you can actually

00:48:33.480 --> 00:48:37.110
see that it's taking two seconds
because of some kind of network

00:48:37.110 --> 00:48:40.700
issue between their
app, some iOS app

00:48:40.700 --> 00:48:43.840
and your backend application.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:45.490
And finally we, of
course, are looking

00:48:45.490 --> 00:48:47.073
to expand the list
of partners that we

00:48:47.073 --> 00:48:49.340
work with for OpenCensus.

00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:52.082
And to that end,
certainly this is

00:48:52.082 --> 00:48:53.790
sort of the big players
in the ecosystem.

00:48:53.790 --> 00:48:56.871
There are a ton of other
contributors, typically just

00:48:56.871 --> 00:48:58.370
open source
contributors not working

00:48:58.370 --> 00:48:59.630
for a particular company.

00:48:59.630 --> 00:49:01.340
OpenCensus, of course,
started at Google.

00:49:01.340 --> 00:49:02.506
We are the main contributor.

00:49:02.506 --> 00:49:04.580
We effectively run the project.

00:49:04.580 --> 00:49:06.080
But in the last few
weeks or months,

00:49:06.080 --> 00:49:07.670
we've also gotten
a lot of support

00:49:07.670 --> 00:49:10.010
from Microsoft,
SignalFx, and Pivotal.

00:49:10.010 --> 00:49:11.600
Pivotal, in particular,
right now is

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:13.820
maintaining a fork
of OpenCensus that--

00:49:13.820 --> 00:49:16.832
basically a .NET copy
of OpenCensus Java.

00:49:16.832 --> 00:49:19.290
And we're there working with
Microsoft right now in turning

00:49:19.290 --> 00:49:22.940
that into an official
OpenCensus for .NET library.

00:49:22.940 --> 00:49:25.040
SignalFx we've been
talking to a lot

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:27.586
about co-developing
that OpenCensus service.

00:49:27.586 --> 00:49:29.960
And they've made some other
contributions to the library,

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:31.770
so we're really
excited about that.

00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:35.210
SolarWinds, Datadog, and Instana
have all provided exporters.

00:49:35.210 --> 00:49:38.140
And with any luck,
they'll move up the list

00:49:38.140 --> 00:49:39.701
and become core contributors.

00:49:39.701 --> 00:49:41.450
But we're also just
really excited to have

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:43.910
them involved to any degree.

00:49:43.910 --> 00:49:47.060
We are always looking to
grow the tent for OpenCensus,

00:49:47.060 --> 00:49:51.080
whether you are a individual
open source developer--

00:49:51.080 --> 00:49:53.550
and there are many, many of
them working on the project,

00:49:53.550 --> 00:49:54.770
as well--

00:49:54.770 --> 00:49:57.170
or you work at another
vendor in this space.

00:49:57.170 --> 00:50:00.262
We're really excited to work
with anyone, like really.

00:50:00.262 --> 00:50:01.970
And so we're really
proud of the progress

00:50:01.970 --> 00:50:03.800
we've made in a fairly
short period of time

00:50:03.800 --> 00:50:07.640
in attracting some big names
of groups that share our vision

00:50:07.640 --> 00:50:09.290
and want to make
tracing and metrics

00:50:09.290 --> 00:50:12.395
and just generally observability
more accessible to everyone.

00:50:12.395 --> 00:50:14.270
And so hopefully when
we show this next year,

00:50:14.270 --> 00:50:15.561
it will be an even bigger list.

00:50:18.040 --> 00:50:19.550
All right, I think that's it.

00:50:19.550 --> 00:50:21.243
Thank you very much for coming.

00:50:21.243 --> 00:50:22.150
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:23.740
Thank you very much
for staying, yeah.

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:26.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

